  "In 2019, a small bakery in a northern European town began selling a pastry it called the “cloud bun,” a soft roll filled with lemon cream and covered in toasted sugar. Within a year, photographs of the bun had spread across social media, and bakeries in at least six countries were selling their own versions. The original bakery, run by a couple named Anna and Mikael Strand, received no money from any of them. “At first we were flattered,” Anna says. “Then we started to wonder whether we should have protected it somehow.”",
  "The answer, in most countries, is that they could not have. Recipes occupy a strange position in the law. A list of ingredients and basic instructions is generally treated as a set of facts or a method, and neither can be owned. Copyright protects the particular way something is expressed — the words of a cookbook, the photographs, the story a writer tells about a dish — but not the dish itself. Anyone who reads a recipe is free to cook it, change it and even sell the result.",
  "Some cooks have tried other routes. A few restaurants have kept their methods as trade secrets, sharing them only with staff who sign agreements not to reveal them. This can work for a closely guarded sauce, but it offers no protection once a dish is served to the public and others can taste it and work out how it was made. Others have registered the name of a dish as a trademark. The Strands could have done this with “cloud bun,” which would have stopped competitors from using the name, though not from baking a similar roll under a different one.",
  "Many chefs argue that this lack of protection is a strength rather than a weakness. Cooking, they point out, has always developed by borrowing. Dishes travel with people, change as they meet new ingredients, and come back in forms their creators would never have imagined. If every recipe could be owned, a young cook might have to ask permission, or pay a fee, before trying a new variation of an old dish. “The kitchen is one of the last places where ideas are truly shared,” says Tomasz Lehr, who teaches at a cooking school. “I would hate to see lawyers in it.”",
